<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Despotate of Dobruja</h1><p class="st-meta">1325 CE – 1388 CE (approx.) · capital Kaliakra</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Bulgarian principality on the Black Sea coast ruled from Kaliakra, a naval power that fought Byzantium and Genoa before falling to the Ottomans.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the breakup of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/second-bulgarian-empire">Second Bulgarian Empire</a>'s authority, a local realm emerged in the coastal region of Karvuna, later called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobruja">Dobruja</a>. Its first known ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balik">Balik</a>, appears in 1346 sending troops to aid the Byzantine empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-of-savoy">Anna of Savoy</a> in the civil war.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balik">Balik's</a> brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobrotitsa">Dobrotitsa</a> (c. 1347–1386), styled despot by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a>, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaliakra">Kaliakra</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/odessos">Varna</a>. He built a fleet that fought Genoese traders and backed claimants to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Empire of Trebizond</a>. The despotate controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> ports and the grain trade, and remained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> and broadly Bulgarian, while also dealing with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tsardom-of-vidin">Tsardom of Vidin</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-wallachia">Wallachia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobrotitsa">Dobrotitsa's</a> son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivanko-of-dobruja">Ivanko</a> made peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Genoa</a>, but the Ottomans conquered the despotate around 1388. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mircea-the-elder">Mircea the Elder of Wallachia</a> held part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobruja">Dobruja</a> briefly before it passed fully to the Ottomans.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/despotate-of-dobruja.jpg" alt="Despotate of Dobruja" width="320" height="277"><figcaption>Image: Alex:D · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Vicina.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1325 CE – 1388 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kaliakra</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Balik, Dobrotitsa, Ivanko</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Bulgarian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Principality of Karvuna, Dobrujan Despotate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1356&amp;focus=despotate-of-dobruja" aria-label="Despotate of Dobruja on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5362 637 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Despotate of Dobruja lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5682 893L5619 905M5682 893L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5619 905h0M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5682 893h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5682 893h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1356&amp;focus=despotate-of-dobruja">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
